A||||4||Bryan Hord||Headlight Motor Problem
Z||000000||Bryan Hord||06-27-99||12:41 PM||||I'm having a problem with the right headlight.  The headlight was stuck in the up position and whenever I turned my lights on the manual wheel on the headlight motor would constantly spin making a lot of noise.<p>I replaced the motor with one from the junk yard and everything seemed to work while I was testing it, then the next day, when I turned the headlights off the headlight went down but the manual wheel started spinning while the lights were off.  I took the motor back to the junk yard and traded it for another -- Installed it, and the same thing is happening.  I also replaced the relay switch for the right headlight.  Does anyone have any ideas?  I'm stumped.  
Z||000001||Bruce||06-27-99||05:38 PM||||You might want to contact Tom McElroy @TMC87@hotmail.com  -He sells a headlight motor rebuild kit for $35. And, maybe he can explain your problem.
Z||000002||theogre||06-27-99||07:45 PM||||I'll assume from your description the is a pre87 car.....<p>You are stripping the gears in the headlight. This is likely either a string of bad motors, or you are not shutting off the motor. A string of bad motors is entirely posible. The limit switches could also need some work. Does anyone know if those are adjustable? a limiter out of adjustment for a specific car could also chew up motors.<p>Pre 87 headlights use a combination of switches in the motor and relays to run the motor. If the limit switch, or the relays are not right, then the motor doesn't shut off.<p>87+ cars use a control box that monitors the current draw of the motor. This box shuts off the motor when it draws above a certain amount of current. The design of these motors also includes shear pins to prevent gear stripping should something jam.
Z||000003||Carrolles||06-28-99||12:05 PM||||The limit switches aren't really adjustable on the pre-87 motors but the contact points could be covered with carbon much like a set of worn out distributor points.  Cleasnthem with an eraser or some fine emory paper or steel wool.<br>It sounds like the gear is stripped out.  You can crack open the motor case and carefully remove the gear and turn it around 180 degrees.  During its operation this gear traveles less than 180 degrees.  So turning the gear around gives you a new set of teeth to use.  Just be sure and put everything back exactly where it came from and don't move the pinion gear or any of the mechanism while you turn the gear around.  Everything should be back in its original position when you are through.<p>Take care,<br>Carroll
Z||000004||Bryan Hord||06-28-99||06:36 PM||||Thanks alot for your help.  I'll check those things out and let you know the outcome.
